1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,240
A recurrent network reads a sentence one word

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:03,836
at a time.

3
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:06,678
Each hidden state waits for the one before it,

4
00:00:06,678 --> 00:00:09,507
so within a sentence, seven words take seven

5
00:00:09,507 --> 00:00:10,469
steps in a row.

6
00:00:10,767 --> 00:00:13,239
Attention already let the decoder look back at

7
00:00:13,239 --> 00:00:16,359
every word, but it rode on top of that recurrence.

8
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:18,789
Throw the recurrence away and keep only

9
00:00:18,789 --> 00:00:21,483
attention. A model built that way,

10
00:00:21,483 --> 00:00:23,949
trained for twelve hours on eight GPUs,

11
00:00:23,949 --> 00:00:26,162
comes out ahead of every published

12
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:29,411
English-to-German system, ensembles included.

13
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:33,033
That is what the paper Attention Is All You

14
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:34,233
Need sets out to show.

15
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:37,457
So what was the recurrence costing?

16
00:00:37,457 --> 00:00:39,806
In this sentence, ran needs its subject,

17
00:00:39,806 --> 00:00:41,347
cat, five words back.

18
00:00:41,633 --> 00:00:44,209
In the recurrent network, cat travels through

19
00:00:44,209 --> 00:00:47,441
five steps of hidden state, none of them in parallel.

20
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:50,892
Convolutional models like ConvS2S do run in

21
00:00:50,892 --> 00:00:53,950
parallel, but a distant pair needs a stack of

22
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:54,775
layers to meet.

23
00:00:55,067 --> 00:00:57,889
Self-attention, a sentence attending to its

24
00:00:57,889 --> 00:01:00,815
own words, links ran to cat in one step.

25
00:01:00,815 --> 00:01:03,419
In the encoder, every word does this at once.

26
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,821
The authors argue that paths this short make

27
00:01:06,821 --> 00:01:08,672
distant links easier to learn.

28
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,359
Up close, each word's vector also includes a

29
00:01:12,359 --> 00:01:14,983
stamp of its position, since attention by

30
00:01:14,983 --> 00:01:16,803
itself ignores order.

31
00:01:16,803 --> 00:01:19,428
From it come a query, a key and a value,

32
00:01:19,428 --> 00:01:20,892
three learned projections.

33
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,718
The query of ran is dotted with every key and

34
00:01:23,718 --> 00:01:26,503
divided by the square root of the key size:

35
00:01:26,503 --> 00:01:27,872
one score per word.

36
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:30,575
Softmax turns the scores into weights,

37
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:32,319
and most of the weight goes to cat.

38
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,816
The output for ran is the weighted sum of the values.

39
00:01:37,033 --> 00:01:39,049
Why divide before the softmax?

40
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:43,497
The model runs several attention heads side by

41
00:01:43,497 --> 00:01:46,900
side, each with keys of sixty-four dimensions,

42
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:49,683
and random dot products of that size spread

43
00:01:49,683 --> 00:01:51,201
about eight times wider.

44
00:01:51,533 --> 00:01:53,891
The authors suspect that pushes the softmax

45
00:01:53,891 --> 00:01:56,789
toward all-or-nothing, where gradients are tiny.

46
00:01:57,067 --> 00:01:59,947
Dividing by eight brings the spread back to one.

47
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:04,991
On translation, training cost,

48
00:02:04,991 --> 00:02:07,857
as the authors estimate it, runs along the

49
00:02:07,857 --> 00:02:08,559
bottom, with English-to-German BLEU up the side.

50
00:02:08,867 --> 00:02:13,976
The base model, the twelve-hour one,

51
00:02:13,976 --> 00:02:14,802
is above and to the left of every earlier

52
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:15,155
system, on the least compute.

53
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:17,878
The big model clears the best by more than two

54
00:02:17,878 --> 00:02:20,238
points, and sets a new single-model

55
00:02:20,238 --> 00:02:21,817
record on English-to-French.

56
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:25,400
The price is paid in pairs.

57
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,512
Every word scores every word, itself included.

58
00:02:28,512 --> 00:02:31,295
A thousand word pieces make a million scores,

59
00:02:31,295 --> 00:02:32,717
per head and per layer.

60
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:35,721
By the paper's own count, that is cheaper per

61
00:02:35,721 --> 00:02:37,933
layer than recurrence only while a sentence

62
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:39,844
has fewer words than its vectors have

63
00:02:39,844 --> 00:02:42,711
dimensions, 512 here.

64
00:02:42,711 --> 00:02:45,255
Training runs the decoder at once too,

65
00:02:45,255 --> 00:02:48,156
with later words masked, but generating output

66
00:02:48,156 --> 00:02:49,881
is still one word at a time.

67
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:54,066
So attention links any two words in one step,

68
00:02:54,066 --> 00:02:56,539
and the encoder computes every link at once.

69
00:02:56,833 --> 00:02:59,247
Scaling the scores, the authors suspect,

70
00:02:59,247 --> 00:03:00,769
keeps the softmax trainable.

71
00:03:01,067 --> 00:03:03,722
That design, trained for twelve hours,

72
00:03:03,722 --> 00:03:05,110
came out ahead of every

73
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:07,019
published English-to-German system.

74
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:10,673
The paper is Attention Is All You Need,

75
00:03:10,673 --> 00:03:12,361
by Vaswani and colleagues.
